1️⃣ HTTP/1.1
Key Characteristics

Text-based protocol

Browser opens multiple TCP connections (6–8) per domain

Requests handled sequentially

Problem

Head-of-Line Blocking

Example:

Request1  → waiting
Request2  → waiting
Request3  → waiting

If Request1 is slow → all others wait

Workarounds used

Domain sharding

Image sprites

Multiple connections

But still slow for modern web apps.

2️⃣ HTTP/2

Major improvement over HTTP/1.1.

Main Features
1️⃣ Multiplexing

Multiple requests over one TCP connection

Example:

Request1 ─┐
Request2 ─┼── same connection
Request3 ─┘

So requests run in parallel.

2️⃣ Binary Protocol

HTTP/1.1

GET /index.html HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

HTTP/2 converts everything to binary frames → faster parsing.

3️⃣ Header Compression (HPACK)

HTTP headers are large.

Example repeated header:

User-Agent
Cookies
Authorization

HTTP/2 compresses them → less bandwidth.

4️⃣ Server Push

Server can send resources before browser asks.

Example:

Client → requests index.html
Server → sends:
        index.html
        style.css
        script.js
Problem Still Exists

TCP Head-of-Line Blocking

If one packet lost:

Packet1 lost
Packet2 waiting
Packet3 waiting

TCP forces order → all packets wait.

3️⃣ HTTP/3

HTTP/3 fixes HTTP/2 limitations.

Major change:

Instead of TCP → uses QUIC over UDP

QUIC Protocol

QUIC = Quick UDP Internet Connections

Runs over UDP but provides:

encryption

reliability

multiplexing

Key Advantages
1️⃣ No Head-of-Line Blocking

Each stream independent.

Example:

Stream1 lost ❌
Stream2 continues ✅
Stream3 continues ✅
2️⃣ Faster Connection (0-RTT)

HTTP/1.1 / HTTP/2

TCP handshake
TLS handshake
HTTP request

HTTP/3

QUIC + TLS combined

Faster startup.

3️⃣ Better for Mobile Networks

Handles:

network switching

packet loss

unstable connections

Example:

Switching:

WiFi → Mobile Data

Connection does not restart.

