Head-of-Line (HoL) Blocking Explained

Definition:
Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking occurs when a slow or lost packet at the front of a queue blocks all the packets behind it, even if those packets could otherwise be processed.

1️⃣ HoL in HTTP/1.1

HTTP/1.1 opens multiple TCP connections.

Each connection handles requests sequentially.

If one request is slow, other requests on that connection wait.

Diagram:

Connection1: Request1 ──> waiting
             Request2 ──> waiting
Connection2: Request3 ──> processed

✅ Workaround: Open multiple connections (but limited, 6–8 in browsers).

2️⃣ HoL in HTTP/2

HTTP/2 introduced multiplexing: multiple requests over a single TCP connection.

Browser sends many streams simultaneously.

BUT TCP guarantees in-order delivery.

So, if one TCP packet is lost, all streams are blocked until that packet is retransmitted.

Diagram:

Stream1: Packet1 lost ❌
Stream2: waiting ❌
Stream3: waiting ❌

⚠️ This is why TCP-level HoL blocking still exists.

3️⃣ HoL in HTTP/3 (Solved)

HTTP/3 runs over QUIC, which uses UDP.

QUIC provides:

Reliable delivery per stream (not per connection)

Encryption and multiplexing

Each stream is independent → packet loss in one stream does not block others.

Diagram:

Stream1: Packet1 lost ❌
Stream2: continues ✅
Stream3: continues ✅

✅ Result: Faster page load, especially on unreliable networks.

Key Takeaways
| Protocol | HoL Blocking? | Multiplexing | Notes                                  |
| -------- | ------------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------- |
| HTTP/1.1 | ✅ Yes         | ❌ No         | Multiple TCP connections required      |
| HTTP/2   | ⚠️ TCP-level  | ✅ Yes        | Packet loss still blocks all streams   |
| HTTP/3   | ❌ No          | ✅ Yes        | Each stream independent, fast recovery |

Example: Loading a Modern Web Page

Resources: HTML, CSS, JS, images

HTTP/1.1: 6 connections max → some resources wait → slower

HTTP/2: 1 connection, multiplexing → faster, but packet loss delays all streams

HTTP/3: 1 QUIC connection, streams independent → fastest, tolerant to packet loss


UDP is Connectionless

No handshake: Unlike TCP (which does a 3-way handshake to establish a connection), UDP just sends packets (“datagrams”) directly to the destination.

No session state: The sender and receiver don’t maintain a connection; each packet is independent.

No guarantee: Packets can be lost, arrive out of order, or duplicated. UDP does not retransmit automatically.

Comparison: TCP vs UDP
| Feature     | TCP                             | UDP                          |
| ----------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Connection  | ✅ Connection-oriented           | ❌ Connectionless             |
| Reliability | ✅ Guaranteed                    | ❌ Not guaranteed             |
| Ordering    | ✅ Yes                           | ❌ No guarantee               |
| Speed       | Slower                          | Faster                       |
| Use Case    | HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, File transfer | DNS, Video, VOIP, QUIC/HTTP3 |

Example Analogy

TCP (connection-oriented): Like calling someone on the phone. You establish a connection first, then talk. If the line drops, you reconnect.

UDP (connectionless): Like sending postcards. You drop them in the mailbox, some may get there, some may get lost, but you don’t wait or check.

✅ That’s why HTTP/3 uses UDP via QUIC: it’s connectionless, lightweight, and allows independent streams so no Head-of-Line blocking occurs.


