Not really — HTTP/3 does not “lose” data, even though it uses UDP, which is inherently unreliable. Let me explain carefully.

Why HTTP/3 (UDP/QUIC) is Reliable

UDP itself is unreliable:

Packets may get lost, arrive out of order, or be duplicated.

But UDP is just the transport layer — HTTP/3 adds reliability on top of UDP.

QUIC protocol adds reliability:

QUIC tracks each stream independently.

Lost packets are retransmitted only for the affected stream, not all streams.

Ordering is handled per stream, so no Head-of-Line blocking.

How It Works

Suppose you request:

index.html
style.css
script.js

QUIC sends each resource as a stream of packets.

If one packet in style.css is lost:

QUIC retransmits only that packet

Other streams (index.html, script.js) continue without waiting

Diagram:

Stream1: index.html ✅
Stream2: style.css (packet lost ❌ → retransmit) ✅
Stream3: script.js ✅

Result: No data lost at the HTTP level.

Key Takeaways

UDP by itself can lose data.

HTTP/3 over QUIC is reliable — data is never lost.

QUIC ensures fast, independent, secure delivery per stream.

💡 Interview Tip:
You can say:

“HTTP/3 uses UDP for speed and multiplexing, but QUIC ensures reliability, so no data is lost even on unreliable networks.”